## Step 4 — commands on the VPS (Debian/Ubuntu, as root)

{{STEP4_INTRO_EN}}```bash
apt update
apt install -y apache2 certbot
a2enmod proxy proxy_http ssl headers

mkdir -p /var/www/tryhackx-media/.well-known/acme-challenge /etc/apache2/tryhackx-media
cd /root/tryhackx-bundle
install -m 644 maintenance.html   /var/www/tryhackx-media/maintenance.html
install -m 644 origin.crt         /etc/apache2/tryhackx-media/origin.crt
install -m 644 {{SITE}}.conf      /etc/apache2/sites-available/{{SITE}}.conf
install -m 644 {{SITE}}-ssl.conf  /etc/apache2/sites-available/{{SITE}}-ssl.conf

# Did the certificate arrive intact? The fingerprint must match the one below.
openssl x509 -in /etc/apache2/tryhackx-media/origin.crt -noout -fingerprint -sha256
```

Expected SHA-256 fingerprint: `{{CERT_SHA256}}`

Port 80 and the Let's Encrypt certificate first:

```bash
a2ensite {{SITE}}
apache2ctl configtest && systemctl reload apache2

certbot certonly --webroot -w /var/www/tryhackx-media -d {{DOMAIN}} \
    --deploy-hook "systemctl reload apache2"
```

Only once certbot says "Successfully received certificate", enable HTTPS:

```bash
a2ensite {{SITE}}-ssl
apache2ctl configtest && systemctl reload apache2
```

If a firewall runs on the VPS (`ufw status` says "active"): `ufw allow 80,443/tcp`.

The Let's Encrypt certificate renews itself (`certbot.timer`), and the `--deploy-hook` reloads
Apache after every renewal.

## Step 5 — check

1. `https://{{DOMAIN}}/` shows the sign-in page and **lets you sign in**.
2. `http://{{DOMAIN}}/` redirects to HTTPS.
3. A track plays and a file downloads.
4. `https://{{DOMAIN}}/media-transfer/health/ready` answers **403** (not `ready`).
5. Stop Apache at home for a moment ({{STOP_HOME_EN}}): the site shows the
